From c9d569b2f9eac8568b226fcacce4e841ffa07255 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Petr Parkan Janda <petronijus@bastla.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 08:23:21 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 77/77] ARM: OMAP4460: steelhead: VC-bridge regulator for VDD_CORE

VDD_CORE (TWL6030 VCORE1) sits on the write-only VC SR-I2C, like the
TPS62361 VDD_MPU, so it has no Linux i2c regulator. Add the same kind
of bridge for it ("steelhead,vc-core-supply"): set_voltage() scales
the core voltage domain through the VP force-update, get_voltage()
reads back what the VP sent (PRM_VP_CORE_VOLTAGE). The range is
stock's CORE OPP100 .. OPP100-OV, 1127..1250 mV; 1250 mV is what the
SGX540 at 384 MHz needs (stock GPU OPP table @0xc0043ec4).

set_voltage() fails (-EIO) unless the VP reads back at least the
voltage asked for, and the VP force-update now returns -ETIMEDOUT when
TRANXDONE never sets after the update (it logged that and returned 0):
a consumer raising a clock on top of the rail -- the GPU's OPPs here,
cpufreq on VDD_MPU -- then stops instead of running fast on a rail
that never rose. A failed MPU step leaves cpufreq at the old OPP.

A root-only, read-only debugfs file, steelhead_vdd_core/vp, shows the
VP readback, the last request and VLIMITTO. The VP's VDDMAX (0x28,
1202.7 mV, stock's too) is below 1250 mV but does not clamp a
force-update: a 1250 mV request read back vsel 0x2c on the unit
(measured with a bring-up write knob that is not kept, so nothing can
move the rail behind the regulator core).

omap_vp_get_vsel() moves to vp.c for both users, and the CORE boot
voltage's failure line says the VC ON value "may" already be written
(a missing scale method returns before omap_vc_pre_scale).

Signed-off-by: Petr Parkan Janda <petronijus@bastla.com>
